WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.052
An authorization pool has two threads.

2
00:00:05.052 --> 00:00:08.580
Payments come in faster than two threads can finish them.

3
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.625
It came from newFixedThreadPool, so its queue has no limit.

4
00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:15.485
Below are four numbers a dashboard would show.

5
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:18.523
What does a caller notice while the queue keeps growing?

6
00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:21.419
At first, nothing.

7
00:00:21.419 --> 00:00:24.186
Every task is accepted, and nothing fails.

8
00:00:24.186 --> 00:00:29.389
But each new task waits behind all the others, so time in queue keeps climbing.

9
00:00:29.389 --> 00:00:31.695
Every queued task holds memory too.

10
00:00:31.933 --> 00:00:32.956
Nobody is told.

11
00:00:32.956 --> 00:00:35.957
Each caller just waits longer than the last.

12
00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:39.480
Now give the pool a bounded queue, with room for four.

13
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:43.163
When it's full, the next task is rejected at once.

14
00:00:43.163 --> 00:00:47.457
The default, AbortPolicy, throws a RejectedExecutionException.

15
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.046
That is what a bounded queue is for.

16
00:00:50.046 --> 00:00:55.194
The caller hears about the overload right away, and can answer with a fast 503.

17
00:00:55.194 --> 00:00:57.149
And time in queue stays short.

18
00:00:57.367 --> 00:00:58.621
So watch these four.

19
00:00:58.621 --> 00:01:03.012
A queue that keeps growing means tasks arrive faster than they finish.

20
00:01:03.233 --> 00:01:08.019
A pool pinned at its maximum is usually waiting on something downstream.

21
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:11.597
ThreadPoolExecutor does not count rejections for you.

22
00:01:11.597 --> 00:01:14.327
Wrap the handler so it counts, then throws.

23
00:01:14.533 --> 00:01:18.956
Of the four, time in queue and rejections are what your customers feel.

24
00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:20.140
One more thing.

25
00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:23.322
The threads are called pool-3-thread-1 and so on.

26
00:01:23.322 --> 00:01:27.931
In a thread dump of a service with eight pools, that tells you nothing.

27
00:01:27.931 --> 00:01:30.073
Name them after the work they do.

28
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:34.229
When the queue is full, you choose what happens.

29
00:01:34.229 --> 00:01:35.468
AbortPolicy throws.

30
00:01:35.468 --> 00:01:41.401
CallerRunsPolicy makes the submitting thread run the task itself, so the intake slows down.

31
00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:45.377
DiscardPolicy drops the task, and nobody ever hears about it.

32
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:52.585
Here is the whole pool: two threads, a queue of four, named threads, and a handler that counts, then throws.

33
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:54.086
The rule.

34
00:01:54.086 --> 00:02:00.852
Bound the queue, decide what a rejection does, name the threads, and watch time in queue and rejections.

35
00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:02.070
Check yourself.

36
00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:06.015
A pool from newFixedThreadPool falls behind during a spike.

37
00:02:06.015 --> 00:02:08.489
What does its rejection counter show?

38
00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:11.552
Zero.

39
00:02:11.552 --> 00:02:15.212
Its queue has no limit, so nothing is ever rejected.

40
00:02:15.212 --> 00:02:18.239
Watch queue size and time in queue instead.
